.Dd November 11, 2026
.Dt READ 2
.Os RavenBSD
.Sh NAME
.Nm read
.Nd read from a scheme-file handle
.Sh SYNOPSIS
See the
.Pa raven-abi
crate (syscall number 0x62).
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The .Nm system call is part of the scheme IPC protocol (see
.Xr scheme 7
and
.Pa architecture/03-system-servers.md ).
.Pp
Filesystem
.Nm
is sequential.  The server keeps the cursor in the open-file description and
advances it by the number of bytes returned.  The wire offset must be zero; a
server returns
.Er EINVAL
for a nonzero offset.  There is no positional read in protocol version 1.
The kernel has no offset argument for this syscall and always sends zero; a
server-side nonzero offset can only come from a raw protocol peer.
.Pp
A
.Xr dup 2
or a transferred reference shares the same description, so a read through either
handle advances the same cursor.  The
.Xr close 2
of one duplicate does not disturb the survivor.
.Pp
On a directory handle,
.Nm
returns packed directory-entry records (see
.Pa raven-proto-fs ) .
A zero-byte result is end of directory.  A buffer smaller than the next complete
record returns
.Er EINVAL .
The server records the directory generation at the first read; if a create,
unlink, rmdir, rename or symlink changes the directory before the next read, the
next read returns
.Er EINVAL
and the caller must reopen the directory to restart.  Directory entries do not
include
.Ql .\&
or
.Ql ..\& .
.Sh RETURN VALUES
The number of bytes read, or zero at end of file or directory.
.Sh ERRORS
.Bl -tag -width Er -offset indent
.It Er EINVAL
The wire offset was nonzero, a directory buffer was too small, or the directory
changed during the read sequence.
.It Er EBADF
The handle is not open for reading.
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr close 2 ,
.Xr dup 2 ,
.Xr open 2 ,
.Xr write 2
.Sh STANDARDS
RavenBSD ABI v1.
